A||||6||Adrift||Alarm Relay question ||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||11-29-2001||12:57 PM||||My alarm has two wires which are supposed to be hooked up to relays. Here are there descriptions:<P>Orange Wire: 300 mA GROUND OUTPUT WHEN ARMED - N.C. STARTER DISABLE (Optional relay required) This wire is provided to control the starter cut relay. Connect the orange wire to terminal 86 of the relay. Connect relay terminal 85 to an ignition wire in the vehicle that is live when the key is in the on and crank positions, and off when the key is in the off position . Cut the low current started solenoid wire in the vehicle, and connect one side of the cut wire to relay terminal 87A. Connect the other side of hte cut wire to relay terminal 30.<P><BR>Orange With White Trace Wire: 300 mA GROUND OUTPUT WHEN DISARMED - N.O. STARTER DISABLE (Optional relay required) This wire is provided to control the starter cut relay. Connect the orange wire to terminal 86 of the relay. Connect relay terminal 85 to an ignition wire in the vehicle that is live when the key is in the on and crank positions, and off when the key is in the off position . Cut the low current started solenoid wire in the vehicle, and connect one side of the cut wire to relay terminal 87. Connect the other side of hte cut wire to relay terminal 30.<P><BR>So here is my question, can I just hook The Orange w with trace wire to the orange wire, and just hook the the starter cable to 87 and 87a, or do I need two seperate relays?||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||11-29-2001||01:25 PM||||You only will need one relay.  The alarm is just giving you two options on how you want to control your starter kill.  I have two recommendations for you:<P>1.  Use the N.C. option, which stands for normally closed.  That means, the only time that the starter circuit is killed is when the alarm is armed.  Otherwise, with the N.O. method, the starter circuit is disabled at all times unless the alarm is un armed, which is ok but alarms can fail or just their power can get disconnected, so what would happen in that case is that your alarm wont provide the ground to fire that relay to connect the circuit. <P>2.  Don't bother with the starter wires!  Most of them are quite large and hard to get into crimp connectors to connect to the relay you'll be using.  Pull down your fusebox, locate the fuel pump wire, and wire up the normally closed way to disable the fuel pump only when the alarm is armed.  The wires are alot smaller and easier to work with in there.  Also, you can hide the relay in the extra space above and to the left of the fusebox (like right above the courtesy light socket).<P>What you need to do is connect a wire that is hot in ignition to terminal 85 of the relay, the orange wire from your alarm to terminal 86 of the relay, and one side of the fuel pump wire (don't know what color) to terminal 30 and the other to terminal 87A.<P>What will happen, is when the alarm has armed (path to ground now available at orange wire), and someone turns on the ignition without it being disarmed (+12V now at terminal 85), the relay will fire and will disconnect the +12v feed to the fuel pump.  So that way, the car will crank but will not start.<P>When you wire it up this way, unless the alarm is giving ground to the orange wire, the relay will not fire and disconnect the fuel pump circuit.  It is exactly the way I have it set up in my car.<P>Email me if you have any more questions!<P>Edited for spelling.<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 11-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||falconhulk||11-29-2001||01:31 PM||||Have to agree fierojoe!  Tried to explain that yesterday.  Fuelpump is the easiest way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Adrift||11-29-2001||01:49 PM||||I already tapped into the starter wire (before I read this reply), it was a lot of work (Had to buy $6 worth of 10gauge wire and crimping stuff), but I got it working. I think I might add a manual button to the fuel pump for extra saftey though... Now here's my issue - I don't want to tap into anything right around the steering column, and all the wires coming out of the fuse box are the space pink/purpleish color! So does anyone know what color the fuel pump wire is?||||reg||1
Z||000004||falconhulk||11-29-2001||02:35 PM||||There is a fuse labled fuelpump.  Trace the wires that plug into the back of this fuse.  All the wires to the fuse block plug directly behind thier fuse.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Adrift||11-29-2001||03:53 PM||||I tried tracing it, but they are all pink and are taped together right afterwords so I can't follow it more then a few inches||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||11-30-2001||01:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B>I tried tracing it, but they are all pink and are taped together right afterwords so I can't follow it more then a few inches</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, my fuel pump cutoff relay is directly above the fusebox before those wires go into the loom.  It would be a PITA to unwrap that loom, trace the wire, and do your thing to it.  If you can pull out enough wire out of that loom to work with, put your relay or cut off switch or what ever you are going to do right there in that small space before the wires go into the loom.  You can easily run your own wires to the relay located there instead of trying to trace the fuel pump wire just a few inches.<P>Glad you got it working, I hoped that my explanation would make sense!||||reg||1
